WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:04.189
Hey Wisecrack!

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:08.960
Disembodied Jared again, here to continue
our epic Philosophy of Christopher Nolan trilogy.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.730
In our first installment, Nolan Begins, we
covered his interpretations of the Self in

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.129
his early films, plus Inception.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:18.340
What does it mean to be a Self?

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.760
How can we trust ourselves and our own thoughts?

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.540
Today, we’re asking:— if there’s so
much conflict already present in the Self,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.960
what are the implications for a community
full of Selves?

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.500
If we can’t even handle our own issues,
is it possible to avoid conflict with other

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.320
people, or is violence inevitable?

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.739
This is the foundational question surrounding
Gotham City in the Dark Knight trilogy.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:44.860
So suit up for some time with the Bat Man—
and we’re not wearing hockey pads.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.649
Welcome to the Philosophy of Christopher Nolan
Part 2.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:51.320
And yep, there’s spoilers ahead.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.339
Behind the face paint and pointy-eared masks,
Gotham is experiencing crisis after crisis

00:00:56.339 --> 00:01:00.889
that affect the functioning of its social
body— the mob is pumping in drugs, the cops

00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:06.170
are helping the mob, the mob is hiring clowns,
and bombs seem to be getting trendier by the

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.170
minute.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.100
Batman may be the focus, but his focus is
on the city he loves.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.640
In the process of saving Gotham, the films
raise questions about the nature of community.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.570
How does a society deal with fear and threats?

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:24.540
How does the presence of a “Hero” like
Batman create or threaten stability?

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.640
Can society-wide conflict ever be resolved
or is it inherently a cyclical process?

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.880
To help us understand Nolan’s social philosophy,
we’re turning to perpetually stoned looking

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.950
anthropologist Rene Girard.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:59.450
Girard claims that the concept of “mimesis”
is both the foundation of all societies and

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.409
the foundation of conflict in those societies.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:06.440
Girard’s theory claims that one of the most
basic aspects of our relationships with other

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.700
people is imitation.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.890
Imitation can be as simple as copying someone
else’s behavior: I see you buying someone

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:17.390
a badass necklace , and I think “wow, great
idea,” and I decide to go buy a necklace

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.390
too.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.390
Everyone’s happy!

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.350
But this can become a problem.

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:27.710
Girard says that imitation of someone else’s
behaviors can, and often does, lead to replicating

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:28.920
their desires.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.540
That’s where I don’t just want a necklace
— I want that necklace, the one you happen

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.590
to be wearing.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.700
This is “mimetic desire,” and it can pretty
quickly lead to rivalry, where multiple people

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:44.060
share the same desire and so compete over
finite resources.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:49.490
Girard claims that our human impulse towards
imitation means that eventually more and more

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:55.020
people will want that necklace and rivalries
will grow and multiply until they are also

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.470
profoundly impactful on the community itself.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:03.349
When one person succeeds in winning the prize,
the other people (or their tribes, or their

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:07.459
surviving family members) seek retaliation
against the winner.

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:12.540
This vengeance is also a form of imitation,
and it basically ensures that rivalries and

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.910
violence will continue and grow.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:20.150
According to Girard, this is the fundamental
reason why there is crime, violence, and war

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.400
in the world.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.881
As Batman Begins... begins, this turmoil is
already established- obviously because, according

00:03:26.881 --> 00:03:30.630
to Girard, this cycle goes all the way back
to cavemen.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:35.459
But what’s important to note here is that
we see individual examples of mimesis occurring

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:41.190
and motivating characters, and most importantly,
later on, we see that Nolan uses mimetic theory

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:46.700
to propose a possible solution to society-wide
violence.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:53.220
Mimesis is most relevant in Batman Begins
as it relates to the individual, especially

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.760
in the creation of the Batman identity.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.380
The original instance of this mimesis is one
man’s desire for the Waynes’ wealth, which

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.160
leads to violence.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.650
Throughout the film, Nolan foregrounds fear
and vengeance as powerful motivators- especially

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.650
in a city as broken as Gotham.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:14.379
We see this again in Bruce’s imitation of
his parents’ killer, Joe Chill in his efforts

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:15.920
to extract vengeance.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.470
Bruce’s time away from Gotham inspires him
to move away from the cycle of vengeance that

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.490
motivated him to avenge his parents.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.910
Instead, he uses imitation as a means of stopping
violence.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:31.130
His fear of bats leads him to adopt the identity
of a bat, but he uses this imitation as a

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:36.660
means to spread the fear to criminals, who
now fear a different kind of bat— him.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.160
The League of Shadows wants to press the reset
button on conflict by destroying a society

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.410
altogether, but that’s not really how it
works.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.750
The problem with their method is that watching
people destroy each other doesn’t actually

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.260
do anything to decrease violence— it inflates
it.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.130
There’s nothing for people to unite around,
so whatever survivors are left after the fire

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:14.860
of London or the Black Plague or the microwaving
of Gotham are gonna still be really pissed off.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.700
So, if the League of Shadows gets it wrong,
what does Batman get right?

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.440
Even while Nolan presents us with the idea
that it is difficult, if not impossible, to

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.210
break away from violence, Bruce spends the
majority of Batman Begins struggling to overcome

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.580
both his fears and his impulse for vengeance.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:40.580
In order to do that, Batman has to transcend
the identity of Bruce Wayne and become something

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:46.440
else, an image in the collective consciousness
that represents justice without personal motivation—

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.510
he has to become a symbol to be imitated,
and it’s this idea of symbols, Girard proposes,

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.460
that can break society out of violence.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:01.360
Unlike the more individually-focused themes
of Batman Begins, The Dark Knight transitions

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.180
to the social consequences of the rapid spread
of rivalries and fear.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.880
After the Mob’s ass-whooping, the city begins
to push back against organized crime, and

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:15.240
the faces of this effort are Harvey Dent—
new District Attorney and Gotham’s “white

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.100
knight”— and Batman, the...wait for it…

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.100
Dark Knight.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:24.260
While the Bat fights with batarangs and armored
lamborghinis, Dent fights crime using the

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:25.850
law as his weapon.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:31.669
The white knight and the dark knight are symbiotic
ideas, and they both inspire imitation.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:36.770
While Dent inspires the rest of the city government
to aim for a safer Gotham, Batman inspires

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:40.180
copycats in poorly-conceived halloween costumes.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:45.270
Still, those copycats are a sign that the
idea of Batman inspires hope among the citizens

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.270
of Gotham.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:50.490
It’s also a sign that those citizens are
copying his tactic of replicating fear.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.680
Or, at leasy, trying to.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.360
But the Mob pushes back, as mobs tend to do,
and the increasing escalation of conflict

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.160
between the knights and the criminals leads
to Maroni hiring the Joker to kill the Batman.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:10.020
Part of the danger of the Joker is that he
only has power because of how afraid the mob is.

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:28.040
Batman’s strategy for fighting crime has
created a monster— one that feeds on the

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:31.380
mimetic reproduction of fear that Batman relies
on.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:35.979
How can you break a cycle that has adapted
to your methods of opposing it?

00:07:35.979 --> 00:07:40.980
Girard tells us that when violence and vengeance
become so great that they threaten the existence

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:45.590
of the society, the majority of people will
quickly set aside their differences in order

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:51.130
to sacrifice an individual, the “scapegoat,”
whose death can restore temporary peace to

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:52.360
the community.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:56.490
Everyone agrees that the whole thing was the
scapegoat’s fault, and bam, boom, burned

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:59.880
at the metaphorical stake, everyone chills
out.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.460
But since it is literally impossible for an
immense social conflict to be entirely one

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:09.240
person’s fault, the “scapegoat” is a
lie, and a pretty violent one at that.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.350
So peace doesn’t last long, and eventually
someone covets thy neighbor’s necklace all

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.350
over again.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:19.199
Girard says that since humans have an innate
impulse towards violence and rivalry, actually

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:24.639
stopping those cycles of vengeance is basically
impossible, with one exception.

00:08:24.639 --> 00:08:29.470
We can replace the scapegoat with a Christ-like
figure who rejects violence and turns the

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:34.620
other cheek, and this could set a new imitative
trend of people desiring non-violence.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.620
Harvey Dent and his campaign for peace in
Gotham appear to fill that role, providing

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.300
a Christ- figure for the community to emulate.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:49.100
Even Batman, who can only fight violence with
violence, supports a world where Dent replaces

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:53.060
him, taking up the mantle of crime-fighting
without the need for grappling hooks.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:06.560
But the Joker, a representation of the true
violence of mankind, shifts the course of

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:11.290
the mimetic rivalry and corrupts our Christ
figure with a few oil drums and some sweet

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:12.080
talk.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.240
Once Harvey Two-Face begins to imitate the
Joker, embracing chaos and going after Gordon

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:21.490
and the corrupt cops, he becomes subject to
the cycles of violence that the white knight

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.050
was supposed to destroy.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:27.660
So, in order to restore peace to Gotham,
we turn to the time-honored tradition of the

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:28.660
scapegoat.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:36.410
Gotham picks an individual, blames him for
the current social ills— in this case, the

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.720
crimes committed by Two-Face and The Joker’s
presence— and punishes him in order to end

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.170
the intense rivalries that threaten the existence
of the community.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:49.610
They don’t kill Bruce Wayne, but they effectively
kill the symbol of the Batman at least for

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:50.610
a while.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:55.790
And during the period where Batman is gone,
there is a time of relative peace and prosperity

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:56.790
in the city.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.880
But, the “scapegoat” is always based in
a lie.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.700
The truth of the situation has to be unconsciously
suppressed in order to achieve the desired

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:07.850
effect— containing the violent impulses
of humanity.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:12.480
However, this lie is unsustainable, and the
problem of this lie is the starting point

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.240
of The Dark Knight Rises.

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:21.600
The third film begins in a time of relative
tranquility, brought on and sustained by the

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:25.560
lie of Harvey Dent’s martyrdom and the scapegoating
of Batman.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.040
This is acknowledged by Gordon

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:35.300
who, just by
recognizing the nature of the lie, threatens

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.280
the stability that depends on it.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.140
But while the Dent Act, made possible by the
demonization of Batman, has effectively ended

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:54.420
organized crime, there are other kinds of
violence still at work in the city— the

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:58.270
social and economic inequalities which define
the third film.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:03.290
As one form of violence ends, another begins—
although it’s hardly fair to say that social

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:08.019
inequality is a new phenomenon in Gotham;
the Waynes were killed because of their wealth.

00:11:08.019 --> 00:11:11.970
But the dissolution of the mob means that
the divides between the “legitimate” rich

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:17.560
and the poor are once again in the spotlight,
and violent rivalry is inevitable.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:37.500
Like the Joker, Bane is focused on revealing
the violence that already exists in ordinary

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:38.140
people.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.460
But while the Joker explores the idea of chaos,
Bane is interested in the concept of the lie.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.880
Which undoes the peace that depended on that lie.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.510
The disruption of the myth of the scapegoat
means that the sacrifice of Batman no longer

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:03.660
serves its purpose, and his disappearance
no longer helps the city.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:17.060
Because Batman sacrificed himself in order
to save the city from itself, his return isn’t

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:20.829
as simple as hopping into a flying car and
catching the bad guy.

00:12:20.829 --> 00:12:25.680
To inspire the city, he has to metaphorically
come back from the dead, which is shown in

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.540
his ascent out of the Pit.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.700
There’s probably a lot of birth symbolism
happening as he climbs out of a hole into

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:36.760
the sunlight, but what’s way more interesting
is this explicit connection to rising from

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:41.740
the dead: in the comics, the underground cave
has another name… the Lazarus Pit.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:46.089
If you’re not up to date with your New Testament stories, Lazarus is a dude who died,

00:12:46.089 --> 00:12:47.760
and Jesus brought him back to life.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.540
It’s pretty much that simple… he rose
from the dead.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:51.160
Coincidence?

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:56.260
Probably not, especially since later on, Gordon
reads a eulogy from A Tale of Two Cities which

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:00.760
describes… you guessed it… the Christ
like-sacrifice of one of the main characters.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:03.290
Nolan man, you're killing it with the literary references.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.450
And this leads us back to Girard’s Christ-figure.

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:11.070
He claims that in order to have real lasting
peace, to remove our society and ourselves

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:15.579
from the cycle of violence, we need to set
aside the myth of the scapegoat and embrace

00:13:15.579 --> 00:13:21.600
a figure who sets an example of selflessness
that forms a new mimetic desire to be copied

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:22.730
by the masses.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:25.940
But even Girard isn’t sure that this is
feasible.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:30.830
If humans are driven by an innate desire for
violence, is it realistic to hope for something

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:31.830
different?

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:36.260
Girard says that even in today’s Christian
communities, people don’t really follow

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:37.399
Christ’s example.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:42.180
Nolan asks this question throughout the trilogy—
is peace actually possible?

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:46.100
People battle their inner monsters at the
same time that they’re battling the monsters

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:49.760
of Gotham, but can either of these ever find
true resolution?

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.100
It may appear that Dent was a fake Christ-figure
at the end of The Dark Knight while Batman

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:59.019
becomes a legitimate Christ-figure at the
end of The Dark Knight Rises.

00:13:59.019 --> 00:14:00.980
But is this really the case?

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:05.839
When Batman takes the bomb out over the ocean,
he saves the city from a very visible kind

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:11.130
of violence— the radioactive kind— but
the peace that follows is also based in part

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:15.350
on the lie that Batman sacrificed his life
to save the city.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:18.490
He gets a statue, and the city gets a new
martyr.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:23.470
But maybe the lie of Batman’s death is outweighed
by another truth— that, unlike Dent, he

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:29.640
really did fight violence with self-sacrifice,
creating the potential for a new kind of mimetic desire.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.360
Anyone can copy his motivations, even if atomic
bombs are in short supply.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.660
And 
we see mimesis come full circle in Joseph

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:48.889
Gordon Levitt’s character, who is revealed
at the end of the film to be none other than

00:14:48.889 --> 00:14:49.520
Robin.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.660
The fact that Robin is introduced and that
Batman left him all of his crime-fighting

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:59.279
tools suggests… but doesn’t prove… that
peace may not last in Gotham.

00:14:59.279 --> 00:15:02.209
So how do we maintain stability in our communities?

00:15:02.209 --> 00:15:07.220
The openness of the ending— Bruce in Italy,
Robin in the Batcave, and Batman all statued

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:12.020
up — could lead us in a number of directions,
which is exactly Nolan’s point.

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:16.519
Is a scapegoat myth or a lie necessary to
maintain stability?

00:15:16.519 --> 00:15:20.930
Can a Christ figure really inspire people
to abandon their violent urges?

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:25.130
Or can society never exist without conflict,
no matter what we do?

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.570
Is there always a future crisis on the horizon?

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:32.660
Ultimately, do the limits of human nature
prevent us from achieving lasting peace?

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:37.100
Or does the human spirit allow us to overcome
whatever obstacles may arise?

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:41.980
Nolan’s later films like Interstellar (and
Dunkirk) give us a peek into the possibility

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:47.260
of how and why we might transcend those limits
by constructing new worlds.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.440
Tune in next time. Thanks for watching!

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:50.200
Peace!

